              "text": "Nitrogen is used in plastic welding primarily because it acts as an **inert shielding gas** that prevents **oxidation (burning)** of the plastic during the welding process. \n\n### 1. It Prevents Oxidation and Burning\nStandard hot-air plastic welding uses compressed air, which contains roughly 21% oxygen. When thermoplastics are heated to their melting points in the presence of oxygen, they oxidize. This causes the plastic to scorch, burn, and degrade. \nBy shielding the weld area with nitrogen gas, oxygen is displaced. Without oxygen, the plastic cannot combust. It melts cleanly and smoothly without burning.\n\n### 2. It Creates a Significantly Stronger Weld\nWhen plastic oxidizes during hot-air welding, the resulting soot, ash, and degraded plastic act as contaminants. These contaminants prevent the polymer chains of the base plastic and the filler rod from fusing properly. \nBecause nitrogen eliminates oxidation, the two plastics melt and blend at a molecular level with zero contamination. This produces a highly secure, structural bond that is far stronger and more durable than a standard hot-air weld.\n\n### 3. Cleaner and Safer Working Environment\nBecause the plastic is being melted rather than burned, nitrogen plastic welding produces:\n*   **Virtually no smoke or soot**\n*   **Fewer toxic fumes** in the workshop\n*   **No sparks or ash** flying from the weld point\n\n### 4. Better Aesthetics and Easier Finishing\nIn industries like automotive collision repair (where nitrogen welding is the gold standard for fixing bumper covers, headlight tabs, and grills), appearance matters. A hot-air weld often leaves charred, blackened, and warped plastic. A nitrogen weld leaves a clean, smooth, uncharred surface that is much easier to sand, prep, and paint over.\n\n### 5. Essential for Sensitive Plastics\nCertain plastics, such as **Polypropylene (PP)**, **Polyethylene (PE)**, and **TPO (Thermoplastic Polyolefin)**\u2014which make up the vast majority of modern car bumpers\u2014are highly sensitive to heat and oxygen. Attempting to weld these materials with oxygenated air rapidly degrades their molecular structure. Nitrogen is practically mandatory to successfully repair these materials.\n\n> **How it works in practice:** To avoid wasting expensive gas, most professional nitrogen plastic welders use standard shop compressed air to keep the heating element hot while idling. The machine only switches to nitrogen gas when the technician pulls the trigger to start welding.",
